vmjail
======

A local Linux microVM sandbox for running an OCI container with explicit
host-directory mounts and host/guest observability. Cloud Hypervisor supplies
the VM, virtiofsd serves the shared directories, and a guest helper runs the
container with crun.

This repository is being reduced from darkaifactory. The distributed control
plane, web interface, remote client/MCP facade and workspace CAS have been deleted. The remaining Go packages compile and pass the default test suite.
The replacement local command wiring is still in progress; Go module paths and
executable names have not yet been renamed.

Retained capabilities
---------------------

- Image pulling and OCI bundle preparation.
- Direct host-directory shares, with optional guest overlays.
- User namespaces, per-VM cgroups, resource limits and process cleanup.
- Host resource logs and guest eBPF file IO/process summaries.
- Guest control over vsock and local VM networking/exec primitives.

Writable direct mounts expose the selected host directories to workload writes.
The VM is the isolation boundary; sharing a directory is an explicit exception
to filesystem isolation. IO summaries aid debugging but are not a complete
filesystem audit.

Development
-----------

The Nix flake and .envrc describe the development toolchain. Existing Makefile
and deployment targets still need cleanup for local operation. Run the default
Go tests with go test ./internal/... ./cmd/...; see PLAN.md for validation
results and outstanding launcher/end-to-end work.

Documentation
-------------

- ARCHITECTURE.md: the retained local runtime and component boundaries.
- OBSERVABILITY.md: host accounting, guest gadgets and diagnostic files.
- docs/usage/storage-paths.md: local state and cache locations.
- CONTRIBUTING.md: development and verification rules.
- PLAN.md: current scope, build status and next steps.

The earlier analysis and deletion reports under docs/ record the reduction
process. They describe their audited revisions, not supported remote features.
Git history retains the original project and its removed documentation.
